{
  "requirement": "The agent harness uses the unsigned integer representation of `NodeId<D>` for the current node, supplied inputs, and nodes in tool results. Built-in tools accepting a node ID resolve that integer in the domain's cache. A malformed value or an unassigned integer produces a tool error before any graph read.\n\nFor example, if a search hit contains `\"node\": 7`, the agent can call `read_node({\"node\": 7})` to read the enclosing node.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.node_reference`.\n\n**Obligations**\n1. The harness uses the unsigned integer form of `NodeId<D>` for the current node, supplied inputs, and nodes in tool results.\n2. Built-in tools that take a node ID resolve that integer in the domain's cache.\n3. A malformed value or an unassigned integer gives a tool error before any graph read.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **Current node and supplied inputs as integers:** `supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport` in `configured.rs` inspects every request sent to the local transport. It asserts the prompt contains `Current node: 0` and `Node: 0`. The prompt is built in `run_with_domain_tools`, which serializes each input node reference.\n- **Nodes in tool results as integers:**\n  - `agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets` asserts `outgoing_edges` returns `{\"targets\":[3,2,3]}`.\n  - `configured_agent_uses_domain_source_tools_in_independent_executions` asserts every `list_sources` file entry satisfies `is_u64`.\n  - The same test asserts the declared `node` parameter of `read_node`, `outline_source` and `outgoing_edges` has type integer.\n  - It also feeds a search hit's `node` and a listing entry straight back into `read_node`. This is the requirement's round-trip example.\n- **Resolving the integer in the domain cache:** `read_node_accepts_a_domain_id_first_supplied_by_the_agent` calls `read_node` with `2`, an ID the agent never saw in a result. It asserts the graph read is exactly `[2]` and the dependency is node 2. The `graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_...` test reads node 2 by integer too.\n- **Unassigned integer:** `read_node_accepts_a_domain_id_first_supplied_by_the_agent` calls `read_node` with `999`. It asserts a tool error `unassigned node ID` and that `graph.reads` contains only `[2]`, so no graph read happened for 999.\n- **Malformed value, before any graph read:**\n  - `agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets` calls `outgoing_edges` with `\"node\":\"unknown\"`. It asserts an `is_error` result with `invalid node ID: ...`, that `lookups == 0`, and that `ctx.dependencies()` is empty.\n  - `configured_agent_uses_domain_source_tools_in_independent_executions` calls `read_node` with `\"unknown\"` and asserts the result contains `invalid node ID`.\n\n**Tools not tested individually**\n- `outline_source` has no test of its own for a malformed or unassigned ID.\n- `outgoing_edges` has no test for an unassigned integer.\n- `read_node`, `outgoing_edges` and `outline_source` all go through the single helper `node_argument` in `tools.rs`. That helper first deserializes the ID, giving `invalid node ID`. It then looks the ID up with `cache().node_data`, giving `unassigned node ID`. The requirement does not distinguish between tools, so these are implementation branches the text does not separate. The helper is exercised through both the `read_node` and `outgoing_edges` dispatch paths.\n\nI found no gaps and no acknowledgments are needed.",
  "references": "3c7e87a0485748029acc443133b7e92cff7aed4a573241cb0e2a092ba7262de6",
